---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Byline from "../components/Byline.astro";
import DualDoors from "../components/DualDoors.astro";
import LegalNote from "../components/LegalNote.astro";
import { LINKS, CANON_PATHS, linkClass, ext } from "../lib/links";
import { withBase } from "../lib/base";

const path = "/nics-is-not-sales";
const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "Background checks", path },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="A background check is not a gun sale. | FFL Intel"
  description="Background-check counts mix permits, used guns, and denials. Treating that volume like unit sales is how a forecast goes sideways."
  path={path}
  ogImagePath={CANON_PATHS.range}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-sky-800 mb-3">
      NICS
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink text-balance mb-4">
      A background check is not a gun sale.
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-stone-700 mb-6">
      Gearfire already said this. Counting what rang is better than counting
      FBI checks. It still will not tell you what people wanted and could not
      find.
    </p>
    <Byline dateLabel="August 13, 2026" read="6 min" />

    <figure class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-stone-200">
      <img
        src={withBase(CANON_PATHS.range)}
        alt="An empty indoor range lane"
        width="1280"
        height="720"
        class="w-full object-cover max-h-80"
      />
    </figure>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-stone-700 font-serif">
      <p>
        For years the trade treated NICS like a cash register with extra
        steps. A monthly FBI count went up, and a production meeting behaved
        as if guns had already left the dock. A check is not a sale.
      </p>
      <p>
        Gearfire said so, in public, on Outdoor Wire, 6 June 2024. Kaleb
        Seymour, their data lead: a gap between the number of background
        checks and the number of firearms sold — “multiple purchases per check
        or denials, which the NICS reports do not address.” Same piece:
        permit applications, used gun sales, transfers. Every Index release
        since has said it again. A background check is not a new-gun sale.
        That is correct.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then they built a monthly count of what sold at the same stores, and
        asked the trade to stop using NICS as the production compass. Fair,
        for what sold. A panel of more than two thousand shops — their About
        line on the 13 April 2026 release — is a better sales number than FBI
        checks. It is still a sample. It is still last month. It still cannot
        see a search that came back empty.
      </p>
      <p>
        Checks are not sales. Sales are not “what people asked for.” A quiet
        NICS month can sit next to a busy used-gun counter. A down Index —
        March, minus 11.9 percent, Outdoor Wire, 13 April — can sit next to
        shop websites full of people typing a model that is not on the shelf.
        Guns.com, 12 March, said local dealer sales on their platform were up
        46 percent in February, with used guns about 31 percent of the mix.
        The Index is new guns. Both can be true. Mixing them is how a forecast
        goes sideways.
      </p>
      <p>
        Checks, sales, and what people asked for are three different numbers.
        More on why checks are not sales:{" "}
        <a href={LINKS.gseNics} class={linkClass} {...ext}
          >NICS vs firearm sales</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
    <DualDoors />
    <p class="mt-10"><LegalNote /></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
